# Docker Commands: Beginner Guide and Reference

This guide starts with the basics, then works up to images, storage, networking,
Dockerfiles, and Compose. Try the short walkthrough before using the later
sections as a command reference.

Replace placeholders such as `<image>`, `<container>`, and `<host-path>` with
real names or IDs. For example, replace `<container>` with `web`. Commands that
manage Docker may require `sudo` unless your user has permission to access it.
Never put real passwords in shell history, source files, or shared examples.

## The Basics in Plain Language

- **Image**: the packaged template Docker uses to create containers. Example:
  `nginx` is an image.
- **Container**: a running (or stopped) copy created from an image. You can run
  multiple containers from the same image.
- **Registry**: a place to download and share images. Docker Hub is the default
  registry when you write an image name such as `nginx`.
- **Port mapping**: connects a port on your computer to a port inside a
  container, so you can reach an app in that container.
- **Volume**: storage that lives outside a container, so important data can
  remain when the container is replaced.

Think of the relationship like this: **image = template; container = created
instance**. Removing a container does not automatically remove its image.

## 1. Install and Start Docker (Ubuntu)

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install docker.io
sudo systemctl enable --now docker
sudo systemctl status docker
```

- `sudo apt update` refreshes the package index; it does not install upgrades.
- `sudo apt install docker.io` installs Ubuntu's Docker Engine package.
- `sudo systemctl enable --now docker` enables Docker to start at boot and
  starts it immediately. On systems without systemd, use the service manager
  provided by that system, for example `sudo service docker start`.
- `sudo systemctl status docker` checks whether the daemon is running.
- Verify the client and daemon with `docker version` and `docker info`.
- Adding a user to the `docker` group can grant root-equivalent access. Only do
  so for trusted users; logging out and back in is needed for group changes to
  take effect.

## Start Here: Your First Container

First, check that Docker is available:

```bash
docker version
```

If the command reports a permission error on Linux, try `sudo docker version`.
If it cannot connect to the daemon, check `sudo systemctl status docker`.

Run a tiny test image:

```bash
docker run --rm hello-world
```

Docker downloads `hello-world` if needed, runs it, prints a success message,
then removes that test container because of `--rm`.

Next, run a small web server:

```bash
docker run -d --name web -p 8080:80 nginx
docker ps
```

Read the first command from left to right:

- `docker run` creates a container and starts it.
- `-d` leaves it running in the background, so the terminal is available again.
- `--name web` gives the container an easy name to use in later commands.
- `-p 8080:80` maps host port `8080` to container port `80`.
- `nginx` is the image Docker uses. Docker downloads it if it is not already
  on your machine.

Open `http://localhost:8080` on the same computer to see the Nginx page. On a
cloud machine, use its public address and make sure its firewall allows port
8080. If the command says the name `web` is already taken, remove or rename the
old container before trying again.

When finished, clean up the test container:

```bash
docker stop web
docker rm web
```

`stop` stops the running process; `rm` removes the stopped container. You can
run `docker ps -a` to see stopped containers too. The `nginx` image remains
available for another run.

## 2. Docker Hub and Images

```bash
docker pull <image>:<tag>
docker images
docker image ls
docker image inspect <image>
docker history <image>
docker tag <source-image> <target-image>:<tag>
docker rmi <image>
docker image prune
```

- `docker pull nginx:latest` downloads an image from a registry (Docker Hub by
  default). Prefer an explicit version tag for reproducible deployments.
- `docker images` and `docker image ls` list local images.
- `docker image inspect <image>` prints image configuration and metadata.
- `docker history <image>` shows the image's build layers and their commands.
- `docker tag` adds another name/tag for a local image; it does not duplicate
  the image data.
- `docker rmi <image>` removes an image by name, tag, or ID. It may fail if a
  container still references the image.
- `docker image prune` removes dangling images. `docker image prune -a` removes
  all images not used by a container; inspect the impact before confirming.
  Avoid blindly deleting every image because other projects may need them.

## 3. Create and Run Containers

```bash
docker run <image>
docker run --name <container> <image>
docker run -d --name <container> <image>
docker run --rm <image>
docker run -it <image> sh
```

- `docker run` creates a container from an image and starts it. If the image is
  not local, Docker tries to pull it first.
- Without `-d`, the container runs attached to the terminal. `-d` (or
  `--detach`) runs it in the background and prints its container ID.
- `--name <container>` assigns a readable container name. The final argument
  must be an image, not a container ID.
- `--rm` removes the container automatically when it exits. Do not use it when
  you need to keep the stopped container around for inspection.
- `-it` allocates an interactive terminal. Use a shell available in the image,
  often `sh` or `/bin/bash`.

Example web server:

```bash
docker run -d --name web nginx
```

## 4. List and Inspect Containers

```bash
docker ps
docker ps -a
docker container ls
docker container ls -a
docker inspect <container>
docker logs <container>
docker logs -f --tail 100 <container>
docker stats
```

- `docker ps` lists running containers only. Add `-a` or `--all` to include
  stopped containers. `docker container ls` is the equivalent modern form.
- `docker inspect <container>` prints detailed JSON configuration, state,
  mounts, network settings, and other metadata.
- `docker logs <container>` prints a container's standard output and error.
  `-f` follows new output; `--tail 100` limits the initial output.
- `docker stats` displays live resource usage for running containers. Use
  `docker stats <container>` to focus on one.

## 5. Stop, Start, and Remove Containers

```bash
docker stop <container>
docker start <container>
docker restart <container>
docker rm <container>
docker rm -f <container>
docker container prune
docker container prune -f
```

- `docker stop` asks a running container to stop gracefully, then kills it if
  it does not stop within the timeout. The container remains and can be started
  again.
- `docker start` starts an existing stopped container; `docker restart` stops
  and starts it.
- `docker rm` removes a stopped container. It does not remove its image or
  separately managed volumes.
- `docker rm -f` forcibly stops and removes a container. Use it only when you
  understand what work or data it may contain.
- `docker container prune` removes all stopped containers after confirmation.
  `-f` skips the confirmation prompt. This is a bulk destructive operation;
  inspect `docker ps -a` first.
- Removing a container does not necessarily remove data stored in a named
  volume. Use `docker volume ls` before removing volumes.

## 6. Work Inside a Running Container

```bash
docker exec -it <container> /bin/bash
docker exec -it <container> sh
docker exec <container> <command>
docker cp <container>:<path> <host-path>
docker cp <host-path> <container>:<path>
```

- `docker exec` starts a new process in a running container. Use `-it` for an
  interactive shell. Minimal images may have `sh` but no Bash.
- `docker cp` copies files between the host and a container. It works with
  stopped containers as well as running ones.
- Changes made manually inside a container are not a reproducible build process
  and may be lost when the container is replaced. Prefer a Dockerfile and
  mounted or named volumes for persistent data.

## 7. Publish Container Ports

```bash
docker run -d --name <container> -p <host-port>:<container-port> <image>
```

Example:

```bash
docker run -d --name web -p 8080:80 nginx
```

- `-p host-port:container-port` publishes a port from the container on the
  host. In the example, requests to host port `8080` are forwarded to port `80`
  in the container.
- `-p 80:80` publishes on host port 80. The host port must be available; on a
  cloud VM, its firewall/security rules must also allow inbound traffic.
- Publishing without a host IP can expose the port on all host interfaces. To
  restrict it to the local machine, use `-p 127.0.0.1:8080:80`.
- `EXPOSE` in a Dockerfile is documentation/metadata; it does not publish a
  port. Port publishing is configured when creating the container.

## 8. Persist Data with Volumes and Mounts

```bash
docker volume create <volume>
docker volume ls
docker volume inspect <volume>
docker volume rm <volume>
docker run -d --name <container> -v <volume>:/container/path <image>
docker run -d --name <container> --mount type=bind,source=<host-path>,target=/container/path <image>
```

- A named volume is managed by Docker and is a good default for container data.
  `-v <volume>:/container/path` mounts it at the path inside the container.
- A bind mount maps a specific host directory or file into the container. The
  host path must be appropriate and accessible to Docker.
- `--mount` uses explicit `type`, `source`, and `target` fields; `-v` is a
  shorter equivalent for common cases.
- `docker volume rm` removes a named volume and its stored data. Check which
  containers use it first; do not remove a volume if its data is needed.

Example named-volume mount:

```bash
docker volume create dbdata
docker run -d --name database -v dbdata:/var/lib/mysql mysql:8
```

## 9. Environment Variables and Databases

```bash
docker run -d --name <container> -e KEY=value <image>
docker run -d --name <container> --env-file .env <image>
```

Example for local practice only:

```bash
docker run -d --name mysql \
  -e MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD='change-this-local-password' \
  -e MYSQL_DATABASE='example_db' \
  -v dbdata:/var/lib/mysql \
  mysql:8
```

- `-e KEY=value` sets an environment variable in the container. MySQL images
  commonly use variables such as `MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD` and `MYSQL_DATABASE`.
- Do not use a real password in a checked-in command or commit a secrets file.
  Environment variables can also be visible to users who can inspect the
  container; use a secrets manager for production credentials.
- Use a named volume for database data so it survives container replacement.
- The image name is conventionally lowercase, for example `mysql:8`.

## 10. Connect Containers with Networks

```bash
docker network ls
docker network create <network>
docker network inspect <network>
docker network connect <network> <container>
docker network disconnect <network> <container>
docker network rm <network>
docker run -d --name <container> --network <network> <image>
```

- User-defined bridge networks let containers resolve one another by container
  name and communicate without legacy links.
- Start both containers on the same user-defined network. For example, a web
  application can use the database container name as its database host.
- `docker network connect` and `disconnect` attach or detach an existing
  container. A network cannot be removed while containers are connected to it.
- `--link` is a legacy feature; prefer user-defined networks and container DNS.

## 11. Build and Share Images

```bash
docker build -t <name>:<tag> .
docker build -t <name>:<tag> -f <Dockerfile-path> <build-context>
docker save -o <image-file.tar> <image>
docker load -i <image-file.tar>
docker export -o <container-filesystem.tar> <container>
docker import <container-filesystem.tar> <name>:<tag>
docker commit <container> <name>:<tag>
```

- `docker build` builds an image from a Dockerfile and a build context. The
  final path argument is the context directory; `.` means the current directory.
- `-t` gives the image a name and tag. Use `-f` when the Dockerfile is not
  named `Dockerfile` or is in another location.
- `docker save` archives one or more images, including image metadata and
  layers. `docker load` restores images from that archive.
- `docker export` archives a container's filesystem, but not its image history
  or configuration. `docker import` creates a new image from an exported
  filesystem.
- `docker commit` creates an image from a container's current filesystem. It
  can be useful for experiments, but a Dockerfile is preferred because it is
  reviewable and reproducible.

## 12. Dockerfile Instructions

A Dockerfile describes how to build an image. Common instructions include:

- `FROM`: selects the base image; starts each build stage. It can appear more
  than once for multi-stage builds.
- `RUN`: executes a command while building the image, commonly to install
  packages. Combine related cleanup with installation to keep images smaller.
- `WORKDIR`: sets the working directory for later instructions and the default
  command.
- `COPY`: copies files from the build context into the image.
- `ADD`: copies files like `COPY` and can extract local tar archives. Prefer
  `COPY` unless `ADD`'s special behavior is needed.
- `ENV`: sets environment variables that remain in the image and are available
  to containers. Do not store secrets here.
- `ARG`: defines a build-time variable, optionally supplied with
  `docker build --build-arg NAME=value`. It is not a secure place for secrets.
- `EXPOSE`: documents the port the application listens on; does not publish it.
- `VOLUME`: declares a mount point intended for persistent or externally
  managed data.
- `USER`: selects the user for subsequent build steps and the default runtime.
  Prefer a non-root user when the application permits it.
- `CMD`: supplies the default command or arguments when a container starts.
  A Dockerfile should have one effective `CMD`; a later one replaces an earlier
  one.
- `ENTRYPOINT`: configures the executable to run when a container starts.
  `CMD` can provide its default arguments. A later `ENTRYPOINT` replaces an
  earlier one.
- `LABEL`: adds image metadata, such as a maintainer or version.
- `HEALTHCHECK`: defines a command Docker uses to assess container health.
- `SHELL`: selects the shell used by shell-form `RUN` instructions.
- `STOPSIGNAL`: sets the signal sent to the container's main process when it is
  stopped.
- `ONBUILD`: stores a trigger to run when the image is later used as a base.
- `MAINTAINER`: obsolete; use a `LABEL` for maintainer metadata instead.

Minimal example:

```dockerfile
FROM node:22-alpine
WORKDIR /app
COPY package*.json ./
RUN npm ci
COPY . .
EXPOSE 3000
USER node
CMD ["node", "src/app.js"]
```

Build it from the directory containing the Dockerfile:

```bash
docker build -t sample-app:1.0 .
```

## 13. Docker Compose

The current Compose plugin uses `docker compose` (with a space):

```bash
docker compose up -d
docker compose -f <file> up -d
docker compose ps
docker compose logs -f
docker compose exec <service> sh
docker compose down
```

- `docker compose up -d` builds or pulls required images, creates services,
  networks, and volumes as configured, and starts the containers in the
  background.
- `-f <file>` selects a Compose file other than the default `compose.yaml` or
  `docker-compose.yml`.
- `docker compose ps`, `logs`, and `exec` inspect and work with Compose
  services.
- `docker compose down` stops and removes the project's containers and
  networks. Named volumes are kept by default; `docker compose down -v` also
  removes volumes and their data, so use it cautiously.
- The older `docker-compose` command may exist as a separate legacy tool. Prefer
  `docker compose` when using the current Compose plugin.

## 14. System Usage and Cleanup

```bash
docker system df
docker system df -v
docker system prune
docker system prune -a
```

- `docker system df` reports disk space used by images, containers, and
  volumes. `-v` gives more detail.
- `docker system prune` removes unused containers, networks, dangling images,
  and build cache after confirmation.
- `docker system prune -a` also removes unused images that are not dangling.
  Neither command should be run without reviewing what data and cached images
  you need; add `--volumes` only if you intend to delete unused volumes too.

## 15. Quick Reference and Safety

- List running containers: `docker ps`
- List all containers: `docker ps -a`
- Follow logs: `docker logs -f <container>`
- Open a shell: `docker exec -it <container> sh`
- Stop a container: `docker stop <container>`
- Show images: `docker image ls`
- Inspect an object: `docker inspect <container-or-image>`
- Check storage: `docker system df`
- Get command help: `docker <command> --help`

Before bulk deletion, inspect the target list and use prune preview options where
available. Never paste production passwords into examples, and remember that a
container's writable layer is not a substitute for persistent storage.
